# PlexOS accounts.
#
# Format: username uid group gid password home shell groups comment
#
# The uid and gid are not free choices. They own every file Plex writes under
# /var/lib/plex and /var/cache/plex-transcode, and /var outlives an OS update and
# survives a rollback -- so changing them orphans a media database rather than
# renaming anything. plexos_types::paths::PLEX_UID carries the same numbers and a test
# pins them; if these two ever disagree, Plex is handed a data directory it does not
# own and fails at startup with a permissions error that names neither file.
#
# No password and no shell: this account exists to run one confined process (ADR-0007),
# and nothing should ever log in as it. `!` locks the password; /bin/false makes a
# successful login useless even if something else grants one.
#
# The home directory is Plex's application support directory from ADR-0009. It is
# created here so that its ownership comes from the same declaration as the account,
# rather than from a mkdir somewhere that could disagree.
# A literal 900, not -900. mkusers reads a negative id as "allocate one for me"
# (-1 and -2 are the only negatives it accepts, and it rejects anything below -2),
# which is precisely what must not happen here: an allocated uid can differ between
# builds, and a machine reinstalled over its existing /var would find a media database
# owned by a number it no longer uses.
plex 900 plex 900 ! /var/lib/plex /bin/false - Plex Media Server
